/** @mainpage

This suite exercises the major IoT technologies against the Test Agent it
runs on, one group per technology, each emitting a measurement chart and,
where it applies, a security audit with a known-bad control:

- @ref iot-mqtt - MQTT publish/subscribe and a broker audit (tsf-mqtt);
- @ref iot-coap - CoAP resource discovery, GET and an audit (tsf-coap);
- @ref iot-lorawan - LoRaWAN frame codec, MIC and frame-counter reuse
  (tsf-lorawan);
- @ref iot-ieee802154 - IEEE 802.15.4 frame codec (the layer under Zigbee,
  Thread and Matter) and frame-counter reuse (tsf-802154);
- @ref iot-mdns - mDNS/DNS-SD service discovery and an audit (tsf-mdns);
- @ref iot-ble - Bluetooth adapter scan and an audit (tsf-bluetooth);
- @ref iot-wifi - WiFi station configuration (tsf-wifi);
- @ref iot-gpio - GPIO/I2C/SPI bus access (tsf-gpio);
- @ref iot-sensors - hardware monitoring, temperatures and limits
  (tsf-sensors);
- @ref iot-watchdog - the hardware watchdog (tsf-watchdog);
- @ref iot-ics - Modbus register access and an industrial-control audit
  (tsf-ics);
- @ref iot-cwmp - the TR-069 (CWMP) message codec, round-tripped with no
  ACS (tsf-cwmp);
- @ref iot-security - the cross-cutting hardening layer: fuzzing a frame
  parser and a fake-clock expiry probe (tsf-fuzz, tsf-time).

The default localhost profile needs no radio, device or daemon: the
pure-codec groups (lorawan, ieee802154, cwmp) and the fuzz layer run,
while groups that need a broker, device, radio or bus SKIP cleanly when
none is configured. Point conf/rcf.conf and the TSF_* environment (or
TA_WIFI_A_HOST) at real hardware and the same tests exercise it.

Every audit plants a known-bad control next to it: the assertion is that
the audit reports the planted defect, so an audit that reports nothing is
the failure, not a pass.

*/
